  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">USAID-Other Africa Grants</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&quot;The purpose of this project is to stop hunting of bonobo (Pan paniscus) and forest elephant (Loxodonta cyclotis) in the Maringa-Lopori-Wamba (MLW) landscape in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), which potentially covers over 20% of the estimated bonobo distribution range. It will build the governments capacity to effectively manage protected areas and to enforce the laws regarding protected species. Sensitization and outreach on wildlife conservation will be conducted at the community level. Activities include: To prohibit poaching in the protected areas of the MLW landscape through implementation of Performance Based Management (PBM). To support ICCN in implementing the eight step counter-poaching model recommended as an integral part of PBM. The eight steps are: (1) Criminal information; (2) Threat analysis incorporating SMART-based information collection and reporting capabilities; (3) Operations; (4) Species security; (5) Physical security; (6) Authority/Jurisdiction; (7) Planning crisis management; (8) Performing crisis management. Perform law enforcement monitoring (LEM) in protected areas using Spatial Monitoring And Reporting Tool (SMART). Bio-monitoring of key indicator species (bonobo and elephant) will be conducted using CyberTracker GPS Field Data Collection technology. Conservation covenants in Quid Pro Quo (QPQ) agreements will be signed with local communities of the MLW landscape to enforce no hunting of bonobo and elephant. Enforcement of DRC laws and regulations regarding hunting and trade in wildlife products at river trans-shipment points and local markets in the MLW landscape (Basankusu, Befale, Bongandanga, and Djolu) will be supported through training..&quot;</narrative>
  </description>
